WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:04.698
Picture a shop with two tills.

2
00:00:04.698 --> 00:00:07.495
Each one prints the time on every receipt.

3
00:00:07.495 --> 00:00:10.891
Till B runs fifteen seconds slow, and nobody knows.

4
00:00:11.167 --> 00:00:13.554
A customer buys a jacket at till A.

5
00:00:13.554 --> 00:00:16.556
Ten seconds later, she returns it at till B.

6
00:00:16.800 --> 00:00:21.096
That evening, the owner keeps only the latest receipt for each item.

7
00:00:21.096 --> 00:00:25.329
By the printed times, the return came five seconds before the sale.

8
00:00:25.329 --> 00:00:26.719
So she keeps the sale.

9
00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:29.990
The books now say the jacket was never returned.

10
00:00:29.990 --> 00:00:31.921
Which receipt really came last?

11
00:00:31.921 --> 00:00:34.039
And why did a clock get to decide?

12
00:00:34.300 --> 00:00:36.624
It's tempting to trust printed times.

13
00:00:36.624 --> 00:00:38.508
Your phone always seems right.

14
00:00:38.508 --> 00:00:42.151
But right means within a few milliseconds, sometimes more.

15
00:00:42.151 --> 00:00:45.228
And computers do things a few milliseconds apart.

16
00:00:45.500 --> 00:00:47.784
Many databases use the owner's rule.

17
00:00:47.784 --> 00:00:53.813
When two writes hit the same item, keep the one with the highest timestamp, and drop the other.

18
00:00:53.813 --> 00:00:55.589
It's called last write wins.

19
00:00:55.867 --> 00:01:05.409
Cassandra, a database that spreads data over many machines, takes those stamps from the program that sends the write, read from that machine's own clock.

20
00:01:05.700 --> 00:01:10.493
So here are our tills: two copies of the payments program, pod A and pod B.

21
00:01:10.493 --> 00:01:16.372
Each stamps its writes with its own clock, and pod B's clock is fifteen milliseconds behind.

22
00:01:16.372 --> 00:01:20.653
The owner's book is the store: one payment's status, and its stamp.

23
00:01:20.900 --> 00:01:22.321
Let's draw two lines.

24
00:01:22.321 --> 00:01:26.721
On top, true time, in thousandths of a second around ten o'clock.

25
00:01:26.721 --> 00:01:29.496
Underneath, the stamp each write carries.

26
00:01:29.700 --> 00:01:32.551
pod A writes captured: the money has moved.

27
00:01:32.551 --> 00:01:35.071
Its stamp is two thousandths past ten.

28
00:01:35.071 --> 00:01:38.253
The store is empty, so it keeps it, and says OK.

29
00:01:39.000 --> 00:01:44.387
pod B reads the status, sees captured, and six thousandths later writes refunded.

30
00:01:44.387 --> 00:01:47.713
This write was decided after seeing the first one.

31
00:01:47.713 --> 00:01:50.839
So it's later by cause, and later in real time.

32
00:01:51.067 --> 00:01:56.492
But pod B's clock is fifteen behind, so its stamp says seven thousandths before ten.

33
00:01:56.767 --> 00:01:58.645
The store compares the stamps.

34
00:01:58.645 --> 00:02:01.588
What does it keep, and what does it tell pod B?

35
00:02:01.588 --> 00:02:02.589
Pause and guess.

36
00:02:05.833 --> 00:02:08.903
It keeps captured, and drops the refund.

37
00:02:08.903 --> 00:02:10.668
And it tells pod B: OK.

38
00:02:10.933 --> 00:02:11.777
Very polite.

39
00:02:11.777 --> 00:02:16.344
The refund was confirmed, it's gone, and nothing logged an error.

40
00:02:16.633 --> 00:02:19.551
pod A writes settled: paid out to the shop.

41
00:02:19.551 --> 00:02:20.975
Stamp, fourteen past.

42
00:02:20.975 --> 00:02:23.011
Higher than two, so it's kept.

43
00:02:23.900 --> 00:02:28.496
pod B writes chargeback: the customer's bank took the money back.

44
00:02:28.496 --> 00:02:29.981
Its stamp, five past.

45
00:02:29.981 --> 00:02:31.395
Lower than fourteen.

46
00:02:31.395 --> 00:02:32.951
Dropped, and OK again.

47
00:02:33.167 --> 00:02:35.302
Four writes, four OKs, two gone.

48
00:02:35.302 --> 00:02:36.770
Look at the two lines.

49
00:02:36.770 --> 00:02:39.172
In true time, the writes take turns.

50
00:02:39.172 --> 00:02:46.377
By the stamps, every pod B write slides fifteen thousandths to the left, behind the pod A write it followed.

51
00:02:46.633 --> 00:02:52.208
And pod B keeps losing like this, until its clock passes the highest stamp pod A wrote.

52
00:02:52.500 --> 00:02:57.990
You might think: fifteen thousandths is a big error, so better clocks fix this.

53
00:02:57.990 --> 00:02:59.102
Let's test that.

54
00:02:59.102 --> 00:03:01.117
Shrink pod B's error to five.

55
00:03:01.400 --> 00:03:06.009
Now the refund's stamp is three past, above captured's two.

56
00:03:06.009 --> 00:03:06.399
Kept.

57
00:03:06.399 --> 00:03:07.415
Nothing lost.

58
00:03:07.667 --> 00:03:08.670
Now seven.

59
00:03:08.670 --> 00:03:10.877
The stamp is one past.

60
00:03:10.877 --> 00:03:11.880
Below two.

61
00:03:11.880 --> 00:03:12.984
Lost again.

62
00:03:13.267 --> 00:03:15.308
So the skew doesn't need to be big.

63
00:03:15.308 --> 00:03:21.666
It only has to be bigger than the gap between two writes where the second was decided after seeing the first.

64
00:03:21.666 --> 00:03:24.291
Writes like that are called causally related.

65
00:03:24.291 --> 00:03:26.157
Here the gap is six thousandths.

66
00:03:26.433 --> 00:03:31.012
Add some thinking time between the writes, and the lab counts the damage.

67
00:03:31.012 --> 00:03:35.026
Ten milliseconds of skew loses four percent of confirmed writes.

68
00:03:35.026 --> 00:03:36.908
Twenty loses fourteen percent.

69
00:03:36.908 --> 00:03:38.727
A hundred or more loses half.

70
00:03:38.967 --> 00:03:40.108
Why stop at half?

71
00:03:40.108 --> 00:03:46.354
By then every pod B write that follows a pod A write is lost, and every pod A write survives.

72
00:03:46.354 --> 00:03:51.659
And over the internet, clock errors of ten to twenty milliseconds are ordinary.

73
00:03:51.900 --> 00:03:53.495
It happened for real.

74
00:03:53.495 --> 00:04:00.480
In 2013, Kyle Kingsbury tested Cassandra with writers that took turns behind a perfect lock.

75
00:04:00.480 --> 00:04:05.948
It still lost 285 of 1,009 confirmed writes, about twenty-eight percent.

76
00:04:05.948 --> 00:04:10.124
The stamps, not the lock, decided which write survived.

77
00:04:10.367 --> 00:04:11.603
So here's the rule.

78
00:04:11.603 --> 00:04:15.180
Never let two machines' clocks decide which write wins.

79
00:04:15.767 --> 00:04:17.770
Better clocks shrink the window.

80
00:04:17.770 --> 00:04:19.023
They don't close it.

81
00:04:19.023 --> 00:04:22.529
The fixes stop comparing stamps from different machines.

82
00:04:22.529 --> 00:04:26.161
Back in the shop: keep every receipt, not just the latest.

83
00:04:26.433 --> 00:04:32.564
In a database, that's one new row for each status change, never overwritten, and you read them all.

84
00:04:32.564 --> 00:04:38.881
Or you make the order explicit, with a write that applies only if the value is still the one you read.

85
00:04:38.881 --> 00:04:40.491
Later lessons build those.

86
00:04:40.767 --> 00:04:42.508
So, which receipt came last?

87
00:04:42.508 --> 00:04:43.814
The clock never knew.

88
00:04:43.814 --> 00:04:45.618
It only knew what it printed.

89
00:04:45.618 --> 00:04:50.159
Two clocks can't put two machines' writes in order, so don't ask them to.

90
00:04:50.433 --> 00:04:51.675
One quick check.

91
00:04:51.675 --> 00:04:52.296
Flip it.

92
00:04:52.296 --> 00:04:56.178
Pod B's clock now runs fifteen milliseconds ahead.

93
00:04:56.178 --> 00:04:57.886
Whose writes get lost?

94
00:04:57.886 --> 00:04:59.128
Pause and think.

95
00:05:02.333 --> 00:05:02.925
Pod A's.

96
00:05:02.925 --> 00:05:06.324
Last write wins always favours the fast clock.

97
00:05:06.324 --> 00:05:09.649
The slow one loses, and nobody gets an error.
